  "THE_COLLISION_THAT_WOULD_HAVE_DESTROYED_CONTENT": {
    "found": "Measured BEFORE the copy ran: the 36 derived paths carry FIVE basename collisions across TEN files, and every pair is `X/` against `X/corrected/` -- the corrected and uncorrected versions of the same product, differing by about 2 KB.",
    "why_flat_naming_fails_badly": "A flat destination would have silently overwritten five of them. It fails in the worst direction available: the survivor's size is plausible, the manifest would have claimed 36 objects while HPSS held 31, and a campaign whose entire quarantine story is which products are corrected would have archived one on top of the other. Same class as BEN-136 (names that are prefixes of one another) and BEN-133 (a silent re-point that returns a number instead of an error).",
    "guard": "The launcher carries a FAIL-CLOSED precondition, not a comment: if a future edit reintroduces flat naming the job refuses to start.",
    "PROVED_ON_REAL_DATA_RATHER_THAN_ARGUED": "Each colliding pair archived to a distinct mirrored path with a DISTINCT md5, so the pairs are genuinely different files and the overwrite would have destroyed content, not a duplicate. Verified independently on the HPSS side: `hsi ls -l` on the unified_throw_cov_4d.root pair returns 545,263,178 and 545,400,036 as two separate stored objects.",
